# Response headers for the files Cloudflare's ASSETS binding serves.
#
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
# https://opennext.js.org/cloudflare/caching#static-assets-caching
#
# ## Why this file exists
#
# On Cloudflare the ASSETS binding answers `/_next/static/*`, `/fonts/*`,
# `/images/*` and the brand files at the root *before* the Worker runs, so the
# `headers()` block in `next.config.ts` never sees those requests — it only
# applies under `next start`. Without this file every static asset on the live
# site was served `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is
# the ASSETS default: a conditional request on every asset, on every page view,
# forever. That includes the content-hashed JavaScript and the four subsetted
# webfonts, none of which can ever change behind their URL.
#
# On Cloudflare, `_headers` is consumed as configuration and never uploaded as an
# asset — it is on Wrangler's ignore list next to `_redirects` and
# `_routes.json` — so `/_headers` is not fetchable in production. (Under
# `next start` it would be served like any other file in `public/`, which is why
# this file contains nothing but a caching policy.)
#
# ## The rule these values follow
#
# **Immutable only where the URL contains the content hash.** A hashed filename
# cannot serve different bytes tomorrow, so a year plus `immutable` is not a bet
# — the browser is told never to revalidate, and a change ships as a new URL.
#
# **Everything else gets a day.** The brand marks and the photographs are *not*
# content-hashed: `hero-1080.avif` keeps its name when the file behind it
# changes, which it will — the photographs are placeholders until the client's
# own are re-encoded (`.agents/FOLLOWUPS.md`, wave 3). A year of `immutable`
# there would mean a visitor who has seen the old photograph keeps seeing it
# until 2027, with no way to push a correction. One day is a ~86,400× reduction
# in revalidation traffic and still bounds the worst case at one day.
#
# Rules are deliberately non-overlapping. Cloudflare applies every matching
# rule, and two rules that both set `Cache-Control` on one file would append
# rather than replace.

# --- content-hashed, safe to cache forever -------------------------------

# Next's build output. Every filename carries a chunk or build hash.
/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The subsetted webfonts. `scripts/build-fonts.py` puts the sha256 of the file
# in its own filename and `pnpm test` fails if a file stops matching its hash,
# so these URLs are immutable by construction. The two OFL licence files sit in
# the same directory and are versioned with the fonts they cover.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# --- not content-hashed: cached, but replaceable within a day ------------

# Photographs and the share card. Same filename survives a re-encode.
/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/opengraph-image.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# Brand marks. Small, stable, but still name-addressed rather than hashed.
/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/logo.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/logo-dark.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/logo-mark.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/logo-mark-dark.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/logo.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/logo-dark.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
